      "learn": "Shelley is the author; Walton is the fictional narrator of these letters. He writes to Margaret, not directly to us as readers. His opening reassurance and imagined discoveries show how he wants this reader to see him. A plan or hope is different from an observed result.\n\nAuthor: the real person who made the novel. Narrator: the voice telling or presenting events inside it. Recipient: the person a letter addresses. First person: narration using I or we. Self-presentation: the way a speaker shows or describes themself to a reader. Qualification: words that limit a claim, such as if or perhaps. Forebodings: expectations that something bad may happen. Ardent: strongly enthusiastic. Amend: change or improve. Prudence: careful judgment about what to do.\n\nLetter I word help: Conjectures: ideas not yet proved. The needle: a compass needle. Fortitude: courage through difficulty. Forebodings: expectations of trouble. The date 17— leaves the exact year unstated.",
